name: processes
boss: Daemon
flavor: I started before you logged in. I'll be here after.
brief: Every running program is a process with a number. ps lists them, kill
brief: sends them a signal, top watches them live.
brief:
brief: kill does not mean kill. It sends a signal, and the default is TERM,
brief: which asks politely and can be caught or ignored. KILL (9) cannot be
brief: caught, which is why it is a last resort and not a first one -- a
brief: program killed with 9 never gets to clean up after itself.
---
q: %list% the processes running right now
a: ps
out: ^PID +USER +TIME +COMMAND$
ref: ps
teach: ps
hint: ps alone shows just this terminal's processes, not the whole system
---
q: %list% processes trimmed to just pid and name so you can scan it fast
a: ps -o pid,comm
out: ^PID +COMMAND$
ref: ps
teach: ps -o
hint: -o picks exactly which columns to show
---
q: a process looks hung -- %list% its threads too, not just one line for it
a: ps -T
out: \{[A-Za-z_]+\}
ref: ps
teach: ps -T
hint: -T adds a row for every thread, not just every process
---
q: {watch processes live, updating as they change|open top to watch processes update in real time|run top so you can watch processes live}
a: top
why: interactive
ref: top
teach: top
hint: q leaves it
---
q: {grab one reading of cpu and mem usage to paste into a note, no live screen|capture what's using resources right now as plain text you can save|log a single snapshot of top's output instead of watching it live}
a: top -b -n 1
out: PID|Mem
ref: top
teach: top -b
teach: top -n
hint: b for batch mode -- plain text, no cursor tricks
---
q: {top refreshes too fast to read -- slow it to every 5 seconds|slow top's refresh to every 5 seconds so the numbers hold still|watch top updating only once every 5 seconds instead}
a: top -d 5
why: interactive
ref: top
teach: top -d
hint: -d sets the refresh interval in seconds
---
setup: something called @proc@ is eating the cpu
q: %find% the process ids of anything called @proc@
a: pgrep @proc@
why: live-hw
ref: pgrep
teach: pgrep
hint: pgrep prints matching pids and nothing else
---
q: @proc@'s name is too generic -- %find% it by its full command line
a: pgrep -f @proc@
why: live-hw
ref: pgrep
teach: pgrep -f
hint: -f matches against the full command line, not just the process name
---
q: %list% every @proc@ pid together with its name
a: pgrep -l @proc@
why: live-hw
ref: pgrep
teach: pgrep -l
hint: -l adds the process name next to each pid
---
q: you started @proc@ more than once -- %find% just the most recent one
a: pgrep -n @proc@
why: live-hw
ref: pgrep
teach: pgrep -n
hint: -n keeps only the most recently started match
---
q: several @proc@ are running -- %find% the one that's been up longest
a: pgrep -o @proc@
why: live-hw
ref: pgrep
teach: pgrep -o
hint: -o keeps only the oldest match
---
setup: another process has a name that merely contains "@proc@"
q: %find% processes named exactly @proc@, not that other one too
a: pgrep -x @proc@
why: live-hw
ref: pgrep
teach: pgrep -x
hint: -x requires an exact name match, not a substring
---
q: %find% every process except @proc@
a: pgrep -v @proc@
why: live-hw
ref: pgrep
teach: pgrep -v
hint: -v inverts the match, like grep -v
---
q: %find% the children of process 1
a: pgrep -P 1
why: live-hw
ref: pgrep
teach: pgrep -P
hint: -P filters by parent pid
---
setup: you've tracked the runaway process down to pid 12345
q: {politely ask process 12345 to exit|send process 12345 the default signal, asking it to exit|ask process 12345 to terminate, with no explicit signal}
a: kill 12345
why: live-hw
ref: kill
teach: kill
hint: no flag means TERM, which the program may catch
---
q: {TERM didn't work -- force process 12345 to die immediately, uncatchably|process 12345 ignored TERM -- give it no chance to clean up this time|process 12345 won't die politely -- send the one signal it cannot ignore}
a: kill -9 12345
why: live-hw
ref: kill
teach: kill -9
hint: 9 is KILL, the one signal a process cannot catch or ignore
---
q: {a doc gives you a signal number instead of a name -- send 12345 signal 15|send 12345 signal 15, the number for the default TERM|you're told to send signal 15, not just the bare command, to 12345}
a: kill -15 12345
why: live-hw
ref: kill
teach: kill -15
hint: 15 is TERM by number, same as the default
---
q: %list% the signal names kill can send
a: kill -l
out: KILL
ref: kill
teach: kill -l
hint: -l lists every signal name kill understands
---
q: {skip hunting for pids -- kill every @proc@ by name in one shot|kill every @proc@ running, by name, no pid lookup first|end every @proc@ running without looking up its pid}
a: pkill @proc@
why: live-hw
ref: pkill
teach: pkill
hint: pkill matches by name and sends the signal itself, no pgrep needed first
---
setup: it won't die to a plain kill, and its name is too generic to match alone
q: {force-kill everything whose command line mentions @proc@|nothing else works -- force-kill by command line for @proc@|kill -9 every process whose command line, not name, has @proc@}
a: pkill -9 -f @proc@
why: live-hw
ref: pkill
teach: pkill -f
hint: -f matches the whole command line, and -9 makes it unignorable
---
q: %show% how much memory this machine has, in a human readable form
a: free -h
out: Mem: *[0-9]+\.[0-9]+[MGKT]
ref: free
teach: free -h
hint: -h scales the numbers to whichever unit reads best
---
q: %show% memory in megabytes
a: free -m
out: Mem: *[0-9]{3,5}[^0-9]
ref: free
teach: free -m
hint: -m reports every figure in megabytes
---
q: %show% memory in kilobytes
a: free -k
out: Mem: *[0-9]{7,9}[^0-9]
ref: free
teach: free -k
hint: -k reports every figure in kilobytes
---
q: %show% memory in raw bytes
a: free -b
out: Mem: *[0-9]{10,13}[^0-9]
ref: free
teach: free -b
hint: -b reports every figure in raw bytes
---
q: {how many processors does this machine have|ask nproc how many processors are available|find out how many CPUs you have to work with}
a: nproc
cmp: ref
ref: nproc
teach: nproc
hint: nproc reports how many CPUs are available to you, not the machine total
---
q: {how many processors exist, including ones not available to you|ask nproc for the machine's total CPU count, not just your share|find the full processor count, ignoring any restriction on you}
a: nproc --all
cmp: ref
ref: nproc
teach: nproc --all
hint: --all counts every CPU, even ones your process can't use
---
q: multiple @proc@ run with different args -- %list% each command line
a: pgrep -a @proc@
why: live-hw
ref: pgrep
teach: pgrep -a
hint: -a shows the full command line beside each match
---
q: %find% the process id of @proc@ in one step
a: pidof @proc@
why: live-hw
ref: pidof
teach: pidof
hint: pidof looks up pids by name in one step, like pgrep without the flags
---
q: a script needs one pid for @proc@, not a list -- %find% just one
a: pidof -s @proc@
why: live-hw
ref: pidof
teach: pidof -s
hint: -s returns only one pid even if several match
---
q: %show% the whole process tree, who started what
a: pstree
why: live-hw
ref: pstree
teach: pstree
hint: pstree draws the parent-child relationships as a tree
---
q: %show% the process tree with each process's pid
a: pstree -p
why: live-hw
ref: pstree
teach: pstree -p
hint: -p adds each process's pid next to its name
---
q: %list% every open file on the system
a: lsof
why: live-hw
ref: lsof
teach: lsof
hint: on unix almost everything is a file, so this shows almost everything
---
setup: you need to rotate @log@ but something already has it open
q: %find% which processes have @log@ open
a: fuser @log@
why: live-hw
ref: fuser
teach: fuser
hint: fuser reports the pids of anything with that file open
---
q: you can't unmount @dir@ -- %find% what's still using it
a: fuser -m @dir@
why: live-hw
ref: fuser
teach: fuser -m
hint: -m targets a whole mounted filesystem instead of one file
---
q: %find% and then kill whatever has @log@ open
a: fuser -k @log@
why: live-hw
ref: fuser
teach: fuser -k
hint: -k sends the default signal to every pid it finds
---
q: %show% the memory map of process 1
a: pmap 1
why: live-hw
ref: pmap
teach: pmap
hint: pmap lists the memory regions mapped into a process
---
q: %show% process 1's memory map with full per-mapping detail
a: pmap -x 1
why: live-hw
ref: pmap
teach: pmap -x
hint: -x adds size and usage detail per mapping
---
q: %show% the working directory of process 1
a: pwdx 1
why: live-hw
ref: pwdx
teach: pwdx
hint: pwdx reports a process's current working directory by pid
---
q: {kill @proc@ by its exact name, not pkill's substring match|kill every process literally named @proc@, no partial matches|end every @proc@ by its exact name}
a: killall @proc@
why: live-hw
ref: killall
teach: killall
hint: killall matches by exact process name, unlike pkill's substring matching
---
q: %list% the signal names killall knows
a: killall -l
out: KILL
ref: killall
teach: killall -l
hint: -l lists the signal names killall recognizes
